+# Decision: Concurrency policy for independent edits to one visit
+
+## Context
+
+A visit is a single plot-visit record — observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes — identified by a client-generated UUID. Workers on 12 shared tablets edit the same visit independently after comparing field notes, and an edit can arrive days after the state it started from was fetched. The record store must define what the *current* visit is when such edits meet, and it must do so without treating arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
+
+Both policies below respect the project's hard invariants, so these are background, not deciding factors: neither discards an original submission, both keep the submitted-to-exported path inspectable, and both are operable by two coordinators. The decision turns on what happens to the edits *between* submission and export — specifically whether the system ever combines two workers' fields on its own. Two undesigned nodes, tablet-sync and review-export, size their storage, retry state, and coordinator interface around whichever contract this node publishes; that is why the choice is recorded at this resolution.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+All criteria are weighted; no gate eliminates either option (both clear the hard tenets above).
+
+- **Protection of scientific intent** *(major)* — Species cover estimates and identifications are a scientific record. A mechanism that combines two workers' fields without understanding them can produce a record neither worker submitted and neither reviewed. Turns on the HLD non-goal *"Automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent"* and the tenet *"Do not silently discard a submitted edit"* (a silent recombination is a subtler form of the same loss).
+- **Review workload** *(moderate)* — How many visits land on the two coordinators' queue. Turns on the tenet *"Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator."*
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** *(moderate)* — Ongoing engineering a policy demands after first release. Also turns on the two-coordinators / no-sysadmin tenet: merge rules are living code the team would have to maintain and trust.
+- **Explainability of an exported value** *(moderate)* — Whether a value in the Friday CSV traces to one submitted visit or to a composite the system assembled. Turns on the tenet *"Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable."*
+- **Export readiness** *(minor)* — How many visits reach an exportable state without coordinator action before the Friday deadline.
+
+**Latent criterion surfaced and kept — reviewer fatigue** *(moderate, cuts against the recommendation)*: a policy that sends unrelated-field edits (a corrected observer name, an added species) to review inflates the queue with non-conflicts. A queue dominated by trivia trains coordinators to rubber-stamp, which itself risks waving through a real scientific conflict. This is the strongest case *for* automatic merging and is weighed explicitly in Selection.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission names the base revision it started from and carries a complete visit. When the base is the visit's current revision, the submission becomes the new current revision (ordinary sequential editing). When two edits derive from the same earlier revision, both are retained as *competing revisions* and the visit becomes *unresolved* — no revision is current — until a coordinator designates the winner. The server never looks inside the fields; the visit is the unit of concurrency.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two workers both fetch revision r1 and edit offline. Worker A's submission arrives and, basing on r1 (current), becomes r2. Worker B's submission also bases on r1 — now superseded — so r2 and B's revision become competitors and the visit goes unresolved. A coordinator compares the two whole visits and designates the current revision; both originals remain stored.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Protection of scientific intent — **strong.** The system never assembles a record; every current revision is exactly one worker's submitted visit, or a coordinator's deliberate choice among whole visits.
+- Review workload — **weak.** Any pair of edits off the same base is review work, including edits that touch entirely unrelated fields.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden — **strong.** Needs base-revision tracking and a winner-designation step; no per-field merge rules to build or maintain.
+- Explainability of an exported value — **strong.** An exported visit is one revision, attributable end to end.
+- Export readiness — **weak.** A visit stays out of export until a coordinator resolves it.
+- Reviewer fatigue — **weak.** Non-conflicting edits reach the queue, so the queue carries trivia alongside real conflicts.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger, noisier review queue for a guarantee that the system never invents a scientific record.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission supplies its base state and a structured set of field changes. Disjoint changes — worker A fixes an observer name, worker B adds a species — combine automatically into a single current record with no review. Only changes that overlap on the same field are held for a coordinator, with field-level provenance showing who changed what. Merge behavior is defined per field kind: scalars (date, notes) last-writer-or-conflict, observer list as a set union, species entries keyed by taxon with per-entry cover handling.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects the observer spelling; worker B adds *Salicornia* at 15% cover. The changes touch different fields, so the server combines them into one record carrying both, with no coordinator step. Only if both had edited *Salicornia*'s cover would the visit go to review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Protection of scientific intent — **weak.** Field-disjointness is not scientific independence: a revised identification in one submission and a cover estimate in another can be auto-combined into a pairing no botanist endorsed, because the rules read field names, not meaning.
+- Review workload — **strong.** Only genuine same-field overlaps reach the queue.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden — **weak.** Per-field-kind merge rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries are living code the team must maintain and trust, against the no-sysadmin tenet.
+- Explainability of an exported value — **partial.** Provenance is tracked per field, so the path is reconstructable, but an exported value may be a composite of several submissions rather than one submitted visit.
+- Export readiness — **strong.** Auto-combined visits are export-ready with no coordinator action.
+- Reviewer fatigue — **strong.** The queue holds only real conflicts, keeping coordinator attention on decisions that matter.
+
+**Summary.** Trades maintained merge rules and the risk of an un-reviewed scientific composite for a small, high-signal review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen: whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on *protection of scientific intent* (major). Field-aware merge scores strong on every workload-facing criterion — it directly answers the reviewer-fatigue concern, which is real and was weighed — but it earns those by combining fields the server does not understand, and the project's own non-goal forbids inferring scientific intent. A merge that pairs a revised identification with another submission's cover estimate produces a record no worker submitted and no coordinator saw; that is the precise failure the *do not silently discard* tenet exists to prevent, in a form subtler than an overwrite. The team accepts the larger, noisier review queue — and the standing duty to keep reviewers from rubber-stamping it — over a mechanism that can quietly cross scientific relationships.
+
+This choice **forecloses** automatic combination of any kind: even an obviously-safe observer-name fix riding alongside an unrelated edit becomes review work. It does **not** foreclose a coordinator manually composing a corrected visit during resolution — that is deliberate human judgment, not inference, and its exact shape is an open question settled with review-export.
+
+**Why the merge approach stays on the table for a cold reader.** It is the correct choice the moment the review queue becomes the binding constraint — if the two coordinators cannot keep pace with the volume of trivial non-conflicts and real conflicts start aging past the Friday export, the workload criteria that merge wins on would outweigh the intent risk. Nothing here is irreversible: whole-visit revisions retain every original submission with its base, which is exactly the input a later field-aware merge would need, so the policy can be revisited without data loss. This doc records why intent protection won *today*, not that workload pressure can never change the answer.
+
+**Obligations this decision cascades downstream** (notes, not owned here):
+
+- **tablet-sync** must persist the base revision ID of whatever visit state it last fetched and send it with every edit; retry must preserve that base and the idempotency key unchanged; and the worker-facing UI must be able to tell a worker their submission produced an *unresolved* visit (someone else had already moved it on) rather than a clean save — without that signal the worker cannot know their edit is now awaiting a coordinator.
+- **review-export** must provide the revision-comparison workflow a coordinator uses to see competing whole visits side by side and designate the winner, must record the coordinator and contributing submissions through this node's resolution operation, and must hold any unresolved visit out of the Friday CSV until it is settled. The queue-size pressure noted above lands here, so review-export's coordinator interface is where the reviewer-fatigue risk is actually mitigated.
+
+Turns on tenets: *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*; and on the non-goal *Automated validation or inference of scientific intent*. Taken knowingly against the *two coordinators without a sysadmin* tenet at the margin — the queue is larger — on the judgment that queue volume is a staffing problem the team can see and manage, whereas a silent scientific composite is a data problem they cannot.
