+## Concurrency Model: Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+The record store treats each submitted visit as an indivisible unit. It never combines fields from two submissions automatically; a coordinator decides what a contested visit becomes.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+A **revision** is a complete, stored version of a visit's scientific fields together with its submitting author, server receipt time, and the **base revision** it started from. The first submission for a visit has no base revision and establishes the visit's first revision.
+
+A visit is in one of two states:
+
+- **Settled** — one revision is the visit's **current revision**. This is the usable state a caller retrieves and the only state eligible to be exported.
+- **Unresolved** — two or more **competing revisions** exist and no revision is designated current. The visit is review work until a coordinator resolves it. An unresolved visit has no usable current state.
+
+### How a submission lands
+
+Each submission names the base revision it started from. The outcome depends on the visit's state and the named base:
+
+- **Settled visit, base is the current revision** — the submission is a clean successor. It becomes the new current revision; the visit stays settled. This is the ordinary sequential-edit path.
+- **Settled visit, base is an earlier (now superseded) revision** — the submitting worker started from a visit state another edit has already advanced past. The incoming submission and the existing current revision become competing revisions; the visit becomes unresolved. Neither is designated current, so arrival order does not crown a winner.
+- **Unresolved visit** — the submission is recorded as an additional competing revision. The visit stays unresolved.
+
+A submission must name a base revision that exists for that visit (or none, only when the visit has no revisions yet). A submission naming a non-existent or mismatched-visit base revision is rejected; it is a client error, not review work.
+
+### Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by designating its new current revision and the competing submissions that contributed to the decision. Resolution returns the visit to settled and records the coordinator's identity, the contributing submission IDs, and the resolution's receipt time. After resolution the visit is again a clean successor target: a later edit based on the resolved current revision fast-forwards as usual.
+
+Every original submission is retained unchanged regardless of outcome — a superseded, competing, or non-winning revision is never deleted or rewritten. Resolution records which revision won; it does not discard the others.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and the plot reference before accepting a submission. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another revision on retry: a retry with the same key and an identical payload returns the original acceptance result, and reuse of that key with a different payload is rejected with the original submission left intact.
+
+The stored original payload is kept separately from any later resolved representation, so the path from a submitted observation to an exported value stays inspectable.
+
+Retrieval of a visit reports its state. A settled visit returns its current revision. An unresolved visit reports that it is unresolved and does not present any one revision as the answer; the competing revisions are available for comparison. This is the distinction between a usable current state and unresolved review work.
+
+## Public Contract
+
+The four shapes below are what tablet-sync and review-export size themselves around. Field-level formats (JSON schema, HTTP surface) are left to implementation; this fixes the semantics.
+
+| Shape | Carries |
+|---|---|
+| Submission envelope | visit UUID, plot UUID, base revision ID (absent only for a visit's first submission), idempotency key, author ID, scientific fields |
+| Current-state response | visit state (settled / unresolved); for settled, the current revision with its ID, author, receipt time, base revision ID, and fields |
+| Unresolved-work representation | the set of competing revisions for a visit, each with its revision ID, author, receipt time, base revision ID, and fields, for side-by-side comparison |
+| Resolution operation | visit UUID, the designated winning revision, contributing submission IDs, coordinator ID; produces a new settled current revision |
+
+Server-assigned on every accepted submission: a revision ID unique within the visit and a server receipt time. The client supplies the visit UUID, the base revision ID, and the idempotency key.
+
+## Downstream Contract Work (cascades to sibling nodes)
+
+These are obligations this node's choice places on the two undesigned nodes. They are recorded here as notes, not owned here; each sibling node will design its own behavior when it is written.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+- **tablet-sync** retains the base revision ID of the visit state it fetched, submits it with each edit, and surfaces to the worker when their submission produced an unresolved visit rather than a clean update.
+- **review-export** provides the revision-comparison workflow a coordinator uses to resolve competing revisions, and holds unresolved visits out of the Friday CSV until they are settled. Export eligibility (a visit is exportable only while settled) is review-export's rule to state against this contract.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy for independent edits to one visit | Whole-visit revisions: competing complete revisions, coordinator-resolved | Field-aware three-way merge (auto-combine disjoint field changes, review overlaps) | Whole-visit keeps each submitted visit intact and needs little merge logic; the merge alternative lowers review volume but can silently combine scientifically related edits the rules do not understand. Full tradeoff in [decisions/concurrency-policy.md](decisions/concurrency-policy.md). |
+| State of a visit when a stale-based edit arrives | Becomes unresolved; no revision stays current | First-writer-wins (keep existing current, log the late edit) | Designating the earlier arrival as current would treat arrival order as authoritative, against the HLD tenet. Both become competing revisions so a coordinator, not the clock, decides. |
+| Revision ID assignment | Server-assigned, unique within the visit | Client-supplied | The client already generates the visit UUID; revision identity must be authoritative and collision-free across concurrent submitters, which only the server can guarantee. |
+| Visit treated as the unit of concurrency | Whole visit | Per-field versioning | A direct consequence of the whole-visit policy; per-field versioning is the merge alternative's model and was rejected with it. |
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy — whole-visit revisions (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+2. ✅ A stale-based edit makes the visit unresolved rather than being silently dropped or auto-accepted.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+### Deferred
+1. **What a coordinator may designate as the winning revision** — exactly one of the competing revisions, or a coordinator-authored revision that manually combines them. The resolution operation accepts a designated revision either way; whether record-store constrains the winner to equal a competitor, or review-export presents a manual-compose option, is settled with review-export when it is designed. Recorded here because it narrows the resolution contract.
+2. **Whether an edit may be based on a competing (non-current) revision of an unresolved visit** — the model above accepts any existing revision of the visit as a base. Whether tablet-sync can even produce such a submission depends on what revision it holds; revisit with tablet-sync.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
